1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,800
This is Doing and Feeling.

2
00:00:02,280 --> 00:00:04,320
Episode nine: The Map of Positions.

3
00:00:04,600 --> 00:00:09,320
In 1978, the philosopher Ned Block
asked you to imagine the following.

4
00:00:09,700 --> 00:00:13,120
Take the population of China,
more than a billion people.

5
00:00:13,360 --> 00:00:18,590
Give each person a two-way radio and one
simple rule to follow, so that together

6
00:00:18,640 --> 00:00:22,040
they implement the functional
organization that describes your mind.

7
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:26,690
Wire the whole network up to an
artificial body, and run it for an hour.

8
00:00:26,740 --> 00:00:31,530
If the right functional organization is
all there is to having a mind, then during

9
00:00:31,580 --> 00:00:34,400
that hour the nation of China
should feel what you feel.

10
00:00:34,640 --> 00:00:38,820
If you would be in pain, then
something, somewhere, should be in pain.

11
00:00:39,520 --> 00:00:44,170
Block found that very hard to believe, and
argued that the burden of proof falls on

12
00:00:44,220 --> 00:00:47,180
the functionalist. Many
people share the intuition.

13
00:00:47,660 --> 00:00:53,470
My intuition says no. But my intuition
also says that a lump of wet meat shouldn't

14
00:00:53,520 --> 00:00:55,410
be able to think, and here we are.

15
00:00:55,460 --> 00:01:00,530
There's a short science fiction story,
They're Made Out of Meat, in which aliens

16
00:01:00,580 --> 00:01:03,180
refuse to believe that
humans think with meat.

17
00:01:03,360 --> 00:01:06,900
I'm not sure our intuitions
about substrates are worth much.

18
00:01:07,240 --> 00:01:10,580
That's exactly the right worry
to carry through this episode.

19
00:01:11,080 --> 00:01:14,460
Every position on this map is
partly an answer to the question:

20
00:01:14,660 --> 00:01:16,600
which intuitions should we trust?

21
00:01:16,980 --> 00:01:21,620
Two episodes ago, Chalmers's six letters
sorted the responses to the hard problem,

22
00:01:21,760 --> 00:01:24,890
and last episode gave the most
radical one a full hearing.

23
00:01:24,940 --> 00:01:27,520
Today we walk the rest of the territory.

24
00:01:27,860 --> 00:01:32,830
For each position: the core claim, the
best argument, the strongest objection, and

25
00:01:32,880 --> 00:01:35,360
its verdict on the question
this series builds toward.

26
00:01:35,620 --> 00:01:37,040
Could a machine be conscious?

27
00:01:37,580 --> 00:01:41,260
At the end, a short, clearly marked
trip to the edges of the map.

28
00:01:42,420 --> 00:01:47,230
Start with functionalism, because it's the
default view among philosophers who work

29
00:01:47,280 --> 00:01:51,020
on mind, and the implicit view
of most people who build AI.

30
00:01:51,960 --> 00:01:54,580
Functionalism grew out of a
problem with an earlier theory.

31
00:01:54,760 --> 00:01:57,723
In the nineteen fifties,
Ullin Place and J.

32
00:01:57,773 --> 00:02:00,160
J. C. Smart proposed the identity theory:

33
00:02:00,420 --> 00:02:02,700
mental states simply are brain states.

34
00:02:03,140 --> 00:02:06,260
Pain is a certain neural
process, full stop.

35
00:02:06,620 --> 00:02:11,020
Then in 1967, Hilary Putnam pointed
out that this was too narrow.

36
00:02:11,260 --> 00:02:16,290
An octopus can presumably feel pain, but
its nervous system evolved independently

37
00:02:16,340 --> 00:02:21,770
and is built very differently, so if pain
is one specific human brain state, the

38
00:02:21,820 --> 00:02:26,680
octopus can't have it. Putnam's
conclusion was multiple realizability:

39
00:02:26,920 --> 00:02:31,300
the same mental state can be realized
by very different physical systems.

40
00:02:31,540 --> 00:02:36,150
So mental states should be defined by their
function, by their causal role, rather

41
00:02:36,200 --> 00:02:37,620
than by their physical makeup.

42
00:02:38,260 --> 00:02:43,420
Pain is whatever state is caused by bodily
damage, and in turn causes distress,

43
00:02:43,840 --> 00:02:46,240
avoidance, and the desire for it to stop.

44
00:02:46,540 --> 00:02:50,740
That's the octopus from episode
two, now doing philosophical work:

45
00:02:50,960 --> 00:02:56,390
complex minds evolving more than once, on
completely different hardware, is one of

46
00:02:56,440 --> 00:02:59,940
the strongest everyday reasons
to find functionalism plausible.

47
00:03:00,480 --> 00:03:03,480
Computational functionalism
is the stronger version:

48
00:03:03,780 --> 00:03:06,440
the relevant causal
roles are computational.

49
00:03:07,300 --> 00:03:12,120
Implement the right computation, in
neurons or silicon, and you get the mind,

50
00:03:12,380 --> 00:03:16,840
experience included. The hardest question
for functionalism is one of grain.

51
00:03:17,140 --> 00:03:19,120
Which functional organization matters?

52
00:03:19,440 --> 00:03:23,160
At one extreme, you could define
it purely by inputs and outputs.

53
00:03:23,900 --> 00:03:28,030
Block demolished that version in 1981
with a thought experiment now called

54
00:03:28,080 --> 00:03:32,690
Blockhead: a machine that passes any
conversational test by looking up every

55
00:03:32,740 --> 00:03:36,920
possible reply in an astronomically
large table, prepared in advance.

56
00:03:37,420 --> 00:03:40,240
Behaviorally perfect,
intuitively mindless.

57
00:03:40,580 --> 00:03:45,510
In fairness, that table would need vastly
more entries than there are particles in

58
00:03:45,560 --> 00:03:50,680
the universe. And whatever large language
models are, they aren't lookup tables.

59
00:03:50,920 --> 00:03:54,680
They compress. That's the whole
point of the training process.

60
00:03:55,180 --> 00:03:58,700
Agreed, and Block conceded the
machine was physically impossible.

61
00:03:58,960 --> 00:04:03,870
His point was that behavior alone can't
settle the question, so a functionalist must

62
00:04:03,920 --> 00:04:06,850
say which internal
organization is required.

63
00:04:06,900 --> 00:04:09,100
Too coarse, and you count Blockhead.

64
00:04:09,440 --> 00:04:14,410
Too fine, down to individual neurons,
and you exclude octopuses and machines.

65
00:04:14,460 --> 00:04:17,860
Where in between turns out
to matter enormously for AI.

66
00:04:18,320 --> 00:04:23,050
The best argument for functionalism
about experience, not just thought, comes

67
00:04:23,100 --> 00:04:24,530
surprisingly from a dualist.

68
00:04:24,580 --> 00:04:29,810
Chalmers's fading qualia argument asks
you to imagine your neurons being replaced

69
00:04:29,860 --> 00:04:34,990
one at a time by silicon chips that perfectly
duplicate each neuron's input-output

70
00:04:35,040 --> 00:04:40,320
behavior. Your behavior never changes,
and you keep saying, I see a vivid red.

71
00:04:40,500 --> 00:04:45,530
If your experience gradually faded as the
chips went in, then at some point you'd be

72
00:04:45,580 --> 00:04:50,440
having faded or absent experiences
while sincerely reporting vivid ones,

73
00:04:50,960 --> 00:04:54,680
systematically wrong about your own
mind with no internal sign of it.

74
00:04:54,820 --> 00:04:59,810
A companion argument, dancing qualia,
imagines flipping between your neurons and

75
00:04:59,860 --> 00:05:01,520
their silicon twins with a switch:

76
00:05:01,720 --> 00:05:06,390
if experience depended on the substrate,
your red could turn blue and back again

77
00:05:06,440 --> 00:05:07,720
while you noticed nothing.

78
00:05:08,460 --> 00:05:13,150
Chalmers finds both scenarios implausible,
and concludes that experience depends

79
00:05:13,200 --> 00:05:15,840
only on fine-grained
functional organization.

80
00:05:16,200 --> 00:05:19,660
He calls this the principle
of organizational invariance.

81
00:05:19,800 --> 00:05:22,480
The objections are mostly intuition pumps:

82
00:05:22,660 --> 00:05:27,310
the China brain, the absent qualia worry
that a functional duplicate might have no

83
00:05:27,360 --> 00:05:31,710
experience at all, and the inverted
spectrum, the idea that someone could be

84
00:05:31,760 --> 00:05:35,120
functionally identical to you while
seeing red where you see green.

85
00:05:36,060 --> 00:05:37,680
Functionalism's verdict on machines:

86
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:41,720
yes, a machine can be conscious
if it has the right organization.

87
00:05:42,200 --> 00:05:44,200
The fight is over what right means.

88
00:05:45,140 --> 00:05:50,650
The second major physicalist family is
representationalism, sometimes called

89
00:05:50,700 --> 00:05:56,030
intentionalism. The idea is that the
character of an experience is fully determined

90
00:05:56,080 --> 00:06:01,450
by what it represents. When you see a red
tomato, the redness isn't a property of

91
00:06:01,500 --> 00:06:06,280
your experience. It's a property your
experience represents the tomato as having.

92
00:06:06,480 --> 00:06:11,430
The main motivation is called the transparency
of experience, a point Gilbert Harman

93
00:06:11,480 --> 00:06:16,190
made vivid. Look at a tree and try to
attend to your experience of it, rather than

94
00:06:16,240 --> 00:06:18,930
to the tree. Most people find they can't.

95
00:06:18,980 --> 00:06:23,870
Every feature they notice, the green,
the shape, the depth, is presented as a

96
00:06:23,920 --> 00:06:28,510
feature of the tree. Introspection seems
to look straight through experience to the

97
00:06:28,560 --> 00:06:32,590
world. Michael Tye and Fred Dretske
built the best-known versions.

98
00:06:32,640 --> 00:06:37,370
The classic objections involve cases where
experience and representation seem to

99
00:06:37,420 --> 00:06:42,060
come apart. One is blurry vision, where
the world isn't represented as blurry.

100
00:06:42,520 --> 00:06:46,380
Another is Block's Inverted Earth,
a planet where colors are swapped.

101
00:06:46,600 --> 00:06:51,450
A traveler there, wearing color-inverting
lenses, eventually comes to represent

102
00:06:51,500 --> 00:06:55,940
colors correctly while, intuitively,
their experience stays the same.

103
00:06:56,360 --> 00:06:59,400
Here's why representationalism
matters for AI.

104
00:07:00,080 --> 00:07:04,710
On one leading family of theories of what
makes something a representation, called

105
00:07:04,760 --> 00:07:09,730
teleosemantic theories, a state represents
what it was selected to track, typically

106
00:07:09,780 --> 00:07:14,600
by evolution or learning. Donald Davidson's
Swampman makes the problem vivid:

107
00:07:15,100 --> 00:07:19,345
lightning strikes a swamp and by sheer
chance assembles a molecule-for-molecule

108
00:07:19,395 --> 00:07:25,150
duplicate of you. On a strict teleosemantic
view, Swampman's states represent

109
00:07:25,200 --> 00:07:30,110
nothing, having no history of selection,
so Swampman would have no experiences at

110
00:07:30,160 --> 00:07:35,200
all. Then the question for a language model
is whether training counts as a history.

111
00:07:35,780 --> 00:07:38,130
Gradient descent is a selection process.

112
00:07:38,180 --> 00:07:40,800
It shaped every weight to track something.

113
00:07:41,120 --> 00:07:43,980
That's exactly what some
philosophers have argued.

114
00:07:44,680 --> 00:07:49,590
Dimitri Coelho Mollo and Raphaël Millière,
for instance, have argued that language

115
00:07:49,640 --> 00:07:54,630
models can have genuinely grounded
representations, particularly once fine-tuning

116
00:07:54,680 --> 00:07:58,480
with human feedback gives their
outputs world-directed functions.

117
00:07:58,760 --> 00:08:02,060
So representationalism's
verdict on machines is:

118
00:08:02,240 --> 00:08:07,290
possibly, depending on whether the system's
representations are genuinely about the

119
00:08:07,340 --> 00:08:12,360
world, and on the further question of which
representations count as experiential.

120
00:08:13,360 --> 00:08:19,030
Last episode's position, illusionism,
belongs here on the map as the strongest form

121
00:08:19,080 --> 00:08:23,300
of type-A physicalism. Its verdict
on machines, in one sentence:

122
00:08:23,760 --> 00:08:28,310
a machine could be conscious in the only
sense there is, if it has the relevant kind

123
00:08:28,360 --> 00:08:32,830
of self-model, producing introspective
representations of its own states for the

124
00:08:32,880 --> 00:08:36,180
right reasons, and not merely
because it learned our words.

125
00:08:37,220 --> 00:08:41,510
Now a family that comes at the problem
from biology rather than computation:

126
00:08:41,560 --> 00:08:48,030
enactivism. In 1991, Francisco
Varela, Evan Thompson, and Eleanor

127
00:08:48,080 --> 00:08:53,030
Rosch published The Embodied Mind, arguing
that cognition isn't the manipulation of

128
00:08:53,080 --> 00:08:58,190
internal representations but the activity
of a living organism enacting a world

129
00:08:58,240 --> 00:09:04,090
through its body. It builds on autopoiesis,
the self-producing organization we met

130
00:09:04,140 --> 00:09:08,830
in episode four. Thompson's Mind in Life
argues for a deep continuity between life

131
00:09:08,880 --> 00:09:15,150
and mind. In 2001, Kevin O'Regan and
Alva Noë proposed, in a target article in

132
00:09:15,200 --> 00:09:19,190
Behavioral and Brain Sciences, that
seeing is a kind of skilled activity:

133
00:09:19,240 --> 00:09:22,840
mastery of the ways sensory
input changes as you move.

134
00:09:22,980 --> 00:09:25,060
And Noë puts the slogan bluntly:

135
00:09:25,540 --> 00:09:29,320
consciousness is something we do,
not something that happens inside us.

136
00:09:29,860 --> 00:09:32,300
Varela also proposed neurophenomenology:

137
00:09:32,800 --> 00:09:36,410
pairing rigorous, trained first-person
reports, including from experienced

138
00:09:36,460 --> 00:09:40,650
meditators, with brain measurements, so
that experience and physiology constrain

139
00:09:40,700 --> 00:09:43,750
each other. That program comes
back in the next episode.

140
00:09:43,800 --> 00:09:46,080
Enactivism's verdict on machines:

141
00:09:46,600 --> 00:09:50,520
consciousness belongs to autonomous,
self-producing, embodied systems.

142
00:09:50,860 --> 00:09:53,560
Today's disembodied models
are poor candidates.

143
00:09:54,300 --> 00:09:57,140
Artificial life-like systems,
someday, might not be.

144
00:09:58,200 --> 00:10:00,760
Next, biological naturalism.

145
00:10:01,140 --> 00:10:07,250
John Searle, who died in 2025, published
the Chinese Room in 1980, as a target

146
00:10:07,300 --> 00:10:11,270
article with commentaries, and it became
perhaps the most discussed thought

147
00:10:11,320 --> 00:10:13,520
experiment in the philosophy of AI.

148
00:10:14,380 --> 00:10:18,450
Searle imagined himself locked in a room
with a rulebook for manipulating Chinese

149
00:10:18,500 --> 00:10:22,300
characters. Questions in
Chinese come in through a slot.

150
00:10:22,460 --> 00:10:26,590
By following the rules, he sends out
answers indistinguishable from a native

151
00:10:26,640 --> 00:10:29,680
speaker's. But Searle
understands no Chinese.

152
00:10:30,220 --> 00:10:34,630
Since a computer running a program does
nothing more than he does, following rules

153
00:10:34,680 --> 00:10:39,290
for manipulating symbols, he concluded
that running a program can never by itself

154
00:10:39,340 --> 00:10:44,520
produce understanding. In his slogan,
syntax is not sufficient for semantics.

155
00:10:44,860 --> 00:10:48,500
The systems reply always
seemed obviously right to me.

156
00:10:49,140 --> 00:10:54,350
Searle is one component. The room as a
whole, the rulebook plus the person plus the

157
00:10:54,400 --> 00:10:57,060
scratch paper, is what
understands Chinese.

158
00:10:57,780 --> 00:11:02,650
Asking whether Searle understands is like
asking whether a single neuron understands

159
00:11:02,700 --> 00:11:05,440
English. That's the most popular reply.

160
00:11:05,720 --> 00:11:10,670
Searle's response was to imagine memorizing
the entire rulebook and doing everything

161
00:11:10,720 --> 00:11:16,430
in his head. Now he is the whole system,
and he still doesn't understand Chinese, he

162
00:11:16,480 --> 00:11:21,130
said. Defenders of the systems reply
answer that he would then be implementing a

163
00:11:21,180 --> 00:11:24,380
second mind, one he has no
introspective access to.

164
00:11:24,840 --> 00:11:29,730
Searle rejected that and every other reply,
including connecting the room to a robot

165
00:11:29,780 --> 00:11:35,240
body. His positive view was that
consciousness is a real biological phenomenon,

166
00:11:35,520 --> 00:11:40,470
caused by the specific causal powers of
brains, the way digestion is caused by

167
00:11:40,520 --> 00:11:44,220
stomachs. A simulation of
digestion digests nothing.

168
00:11:44,480 --> 00:11:48,260
A computer simulation of a
rainstorm leaves everyone dry.

169
00:11:48,960 --> 00:11:52,980
Functionalists have a reply to that last
line, and it's worth carrying forward.

170
00:11:53,240 --> 00:11:58,720
A simulated storm leaves you dry, but a
simulated calculator really does calculate.

171
00:11:59,180 --> 00:12:02,860
Some things are what they do, and
some things are what they're made of.

172
00:12:03,160 --> 00:12:07,790
The question is which kind of thing a mind
is, and that question will come back in

173
00:12:07,840 --> 00:12:09,510
episodes twelve and thirteen.

174
00:12:09,560 --> 00:12:14,750
The most developed contemporary version
comes from the neuroscientist Anil Seth, who

175
00:12:14,800 --> 00:12:19,010
argues that consciousness may depend on
properties living systems have and today's

176
00:12:19,060 --> 00:12:23,450
computers lack, such as metabolism
and continuous self-production.

177
00:12:23,500 --> 00:12:26,340
Episode thirteen gives
that case its full hearing.

178
00:12:26,520 --> 00:12:28,480
The short verdict on machines:

179
00:12:28,860 --> 00:12:34,070
unlikely on current paths, more plausible
only as AI becomes more brain-like or

180
00:12:34,120 --> 00:12:40,880
life-like. Integrated information theory,
or I.I.T., sits uneasily on the map.

181
00:12:41,180 --> 00:12:45,690
It's a scientific theory with radical
philosophical commitments, so here's the

182
00:12:45,740 --> 00:12:48,800
philosophy, and in episode
eleven, the science.

183
00:12:49,400 --> 00:12:54,410
I.I.T., developed by Giulio Tononi and
championed by Christof Koch, starts from

184
00:12:54,460 --> 00:12:56,220
experience rather than from the brain.

185
00:12:56,520 --> 00:13:00,280
It identifies axioms,
properties every experience has:

186
00:13:00,500 --> 00:13:02,760
it exists intrinsically, for itself.

187
00:13:03,320 --> 00:13:05,920
It's specific, this experience
rather than another.

188
00:13:06,140 --> 00:13:09,580
It's unified, not decomposable
into independent parts.

189
00:13:09,860 --> 00:13:12,800
It's definite, with particular
borders and contents.

190
00:13:13,020 --> 00:13:16,320
And it's structured, composed
of distinctions and relations.

191
00:13:16,900 --> 00:13:21,730
Then it asks what a physical system must
be like to account for each, and answers in

192
00:13:21,780 --> 00:13:23,260
terms of cause-effect power:

193
00:13:23,480 --> 00:13:28,640
how much the system's current state constrains
its own past and future, as a whole,

194
00:13:28,960 --> 00:13:30,790
beyond what its parts do independently.

195
00:13:30,840 --> 00:13:34,720
That irreducibility is measured
by a quantity called phi.

196
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:36,770
The identity claim is bold:

197
00:13:36,820 --> 00:13:40,920
an experience simply is a maximally
irreducible cause-effect structure.

198
00:13:41,340 --> 00:13:42,820
Three implications follow.

199
00:13:43,260 --> 00:13:45,800
First, consciousness is
graded and widespread:

200
00:13:46,120 --> 00:13:47,840
simple systems can have a little.

201
00:13:48,380 --> 00:13:50,180
Second, an exclusion principle:

202
00:13:50,560 --> 00:13:53,080
only local maxima of
integration are conscious.

203
00:13:53,560 --> 00:13:58,270
That means a collection of people, like
a nation, isn't conscious, because its

204
00:13:58,320 --> 00:14:00,520
members are far more
integrated than the whole.

205
00:14:00,780 --> 00:14:02,710
And third, the verdict that makes I.I.T.

206
00:14:02,760 --> 00:14:07,740
famous in AI debates: what matters is the
physical causal structure of the hardware,

207
00:14:08,120 --> 00:14:12,550
not the software. A conventional digital
computer, with its clocked, modular

208
00:14:12,600 --> 00:14:17,830
architecture, has negligible integrated
information whatever program it runs, even a

209
00:14:17,880 --> 00:14:22,810
perfect brain simulation. That's the
subtitle of Koch's book, The Feeling of Life

210
00:14:22,860 --> 00:14:26,680
Itself: consciousness is
widespread but can't be computed.

211
00:14:27,040 --> 00:14:34,170
So on I.I.T., a perfect simulation of my
brain on a GPU cluster is unconscious, but

212
00:14:34,220 --> 00:14:39,020
a large enough grid of simple logic
gates might be more conscious than I am.

213
00:14:39,340 --> 00:14:44,640
Yes. The computer scientist Scott Aaronson
raised examples like that as a reductio,

214
00:14:45,160 --> 00:14:48,140
and Tononi accepted that a
suitable grid could be conscious.

215
00:14:48,940 --> 00:14:50,590
I.I.T.'s verdict on machines:

216
00:14:50,640 --> 00:14:54,220
not conventional digital
computers, whatever their behavior.

217
00:14:54,760 --> 00:14:59,210
Hardware built with the right physical
feedback structure, such as some neuromorphic

218
00:14:59,260 --> 00:15:01,480
designs, could in principle be different.

219
00:15:02,460 --> 00:15:06,500
Then type-F: Russellian
monism and panpsychism.

220
00:15:06,740 --> 00:15:12,890
In 1927, Bertrand Russell argued, and
Arthur Eddington soon agreed, that physics

221
00:15:12,940 --> 00:15:14,620
tells us only about structure:

222
00:15:14,900 --> 00:15:19,460
how things relate and behave mathematically,
never what they are intrinsically.

223
00:15:19,820 --> 00:15:24,810
Mass and charge are defined by what they
do, which leaves open what it is that does

224
00:15:24,860 --> 00:15:29,890
the doing. The Russellian monist
proposal is that the intrinsic nature is

225
00:15:29,940 --> 00:15:35,090
experiential, or proto-experiential, and
that's how consciousness fits into the

226
00:15:35,140 --> 00:15:37,160
physical world without breaking physics.

227
00:15:37,780 --> 00:15:42,410
Panpsychism follows if you think those
intrinsic natures are themselves tiny

228
00:15:42,460 --> 00:15:49,650
experiences. In 2006, in a journal symposium
that drew a long list of replies, Galen

229
00:15:49,700 --> 00:15:55,350
Strawson argued that real physicalism
entails panpsychism, since experience can't

230
00:15:55,400 --> 00:15:57,940
emerge from wholly non-experiential stuff.

231
00:15:58,280 --> 00:16:03,150
Philip Goff's Galileo's Error argues
that modern science began by deliberately

232
00:16:03,200 --> 00:16:08,390
setting sensory qualities aside to make
nature mathematical, and that consciousness

233
00:16:08,440 --> 00:16:10,800
is the bill for that decision coming due.

234
00:16:11,020 --> 00:16:13,740
The great problem is
the combination problem.

235
00:16:14,180 --> 00:16:16,480
William James stated it in 1890:

236
00:16:16,900 --> 00:16:21,930
take a sentence of twelve words, give one
word to each of twelve men, and no one

237
00:16:21,980 --> 00:16:23,600
will be aware of the whole sentence.

238
00:16:24,200 --> 00:16:28,740
How do micro-experiences combine
into one unified subject like you?

239
00:16:29,020 --> 00:16:33,450
Chalmers has distinguished several versions
of the problem, the hardest being how

240
00:16:33,500 --> 00:16:35,690
subjects could sum into a new subject.

241
00:16:35,740 --> 00:16:39,300
Some panpsychists respond
with cosmopsychism:

242
00:16:39,460 --> 00:16:44,850
the universe as a whole is the fundamental
subject, and individual minds are aspects

243
00:16:44,900 --> 00:16:50,170
of it. The physicist Sean Carroll gave the
sharpest scientific objection, in a long

244
00:16:50,220 --> 00:16:55,180
conversation with Goff on his podcast
Mindscape and later in a 2021 paper.

245
00:16:55,780 --> 00:17:00,470
The laws governing everyday matter, what
Carroll calls the Core Theory, are tested

246
00:17:00,520 --> 00:17:02,060
to extraordinary precision.

247
00:17:02,260 --> 00:17:07,130
If consciousness made any difference to
what particles do, those laws would be wrong

248
00:17:07,180 --> 00:17:08,380
somewhere we'd have noticed.

249
00:17:08,480 --> 00:17:12,730
And if it makes no difference, it's hard
to see how it could explain why we talk

250
00:17:12,780 --> 00:17:17,650
about consciousness. Goff's reply is that
panpsychism doesn't change the equations

251
00:17:17,700 --> 00:17:22,520
at all. It's a claim about the intrinsic
nature of whatever the equations describe.

252
00:17:23,240 --> 00:17:27,490
Carroll's rejoinder is that a nature
that adds nothing to the dynamics does no

253
00:17:27,540 --> 00:17:31,700
explanatory work. Each side thinks
the other has changed the subject.

254
00:17:32,100 --> 00:17:37,360
If electrons have tiny experiences,
does my GPU get a bigger one?

255
00:17:37,680 --> 00:17:40,840
Not automatically, and
that's often misunderstood.

256
00:17:41,260 --> 00:17:46,370
Most panpsychists think a rock is a mere
aggregate of micro-experiences without

257
00:17:46,420 --> 00:17:51,850
being a subject itself. Whether a computer
is more like a brain or more like a rock

258
00:17:51,900 --> 00:17:55,360
depends on the combination
principle, which nobody has.

259
00:17:55,560 --> 00:17:59,220
So panpsychism's verdict on
machines is genuinely open.

260
00:18:00,140 --> 00:18:02,420
Four more positions, more briefly.

261
00:18:02,920 --> 00:18:07,870
Idealism says experience is all there
is, and matter is how mind appears from

262
00:18:07,920 --> 00:18:13,410
outside. Bernardo Kastrup's analytic
idealism proposes a single universal

263
00:18:13,460 --> 00:18:19,010
consciousness that dissociates into
individual minds, by analogy with dissociative

264
00:18:19,060 --> 00:18:23,510
identity disorder, where one person can
present several distinct identities.

265
00:18:23,560 --> 00:18:28,270
On that view, the question about a machine
is whether it is a separate dissociated

266
00:18:28,320 --> 00:18:32,840
mind of its own, and nothing in the
theory suggests that a computer would be.

267
00:18:33,300 --> 00:18:35,480
Dualism comes in two main forms.

268
00:18:36,300 --> 00:18:41,330
Interactionist dualism, Descartes's view,
holds that a non-physical mind pushes the

269
00:18:41,380 --> 00:18:47,090
body around. It faces the problem Princess
Elisabeth of Bohemia raised in letters to

270
00:18:47,140 --> 00:18:51,870
Descartes in 1643: how could something
with no extension move matter?

271
00:18:51,920 --> 00:18:55,360
Modern physics adds the
problem of causal closure.

272
00:18:55,820 --> 00:19:00,930
Naturalistic property dualism, Chalmers's
view, adds experience as a fundamental

273
00:19:00,980 --> 00:19:05,380
feature tied to physical organization
by laws we haven't yet found.

274
00:19:05,780 --> 00:19:08,020
Remember the fading qualia argument:

275
00:19:08,300 --> 00:19:13,250
Chalmers thinks those laws link experience
to functional organization, so his

276
00:19:13,300 --> 00:19:16,840
dualism, surprisingly, says
yes to machine consciousness.

277
00:19:17,120 --> 00:19:22,590
The neighboring view, epiphenomenalism,
makes experience causally idle, which raises

278
00:19:22,640 --> 00:19:25,080
a nasty puzzle, the meta-problem again:

279
00:19:25,440 --> 00:19:28,760
if experience does nothing, why
do we keep talking about it?

280
00:19:29,480 --> 00:19:35,430
Mysterianism, from Colin McGinn in 1989,
holds that there is a natural answer, but

281
00:19:35,480 --> 00:19:40,480
that human minds are cognitively closed to
it, the way a dog is closed to calculus.

282
00:19:40,800 --> 00:19:45,890
A neighboring stance says phenomenal
consciousness lies outside the reach of science

283
00:19:45,940 --> 00:19:49,640
altogether. The verdict on
machines is that we may never know.

284
00:19:49,800 --> 00:19:55,630
And the quantum route. Roger Penrose argued
in The Emperor's New Mind and Shadows of

285
00:19:55,680 --> 00:20:00,970
the Mind that human mathematical
understanding isn't algorithmic, using Gödel's

286
00:20:01,020 --> 00:20:05,770
incompleteness theorems: we can supposedly
see the truth of statements that any

287
00:20:05,820 --> 00:20:07,940
given formal system can't prove.

288
00:20:08,400 --> 00:20:13,530
Everyone I know in machine learning thinks
the Gödel argument is wrong, but they

289
00:20:13,580 --> 00:20:16,570
usually can't say why. Here's the why.

290
00:20:16,620 --> 00:20:21,270
The argument needs us to know that our
own reasoning is consistent, and we don't.

291
00:20:21,320 --> 00:20:23,720
Most logicians reject it on those grounds.

292
00:20:23,920 --> 00:20:26,250
But Penrose paired it
with a physical proposal:

293
00:20:26,300 --> 00:20:30,800
wave-function collapse caused by gravity,
which he calls objective reduction.

294
00:20:31,100 --> 00:20:35,990
With the anesthesiologist Stuart Hameroff,
he proposed that orchestrated versions of

295
00:20:36,040 --> 00:20:40,440
this collapse happen in microtubules,
the protein scaffolding inside neurons.

296
00:20:40,960 --> 00:20:44,750
Hence Orch O.R. The main
objection is decoherence.

297
00:20:44,800 --> 00:20:50,850
The brain is warm and wet, and in 2000
the physicist Max Tegmark calculated that

298
00:20:50,900 --> 00:20:55,160
quantum coherence there should vanish in
far less than a trillionth of a second,

299
00:20:55,400 --> 00:20:59,040
while neurons work on timescales
of thousandths of a second.

300
00:20:59,700 --> 00:21:02,140
Hameroff and colleagues
disputed the calculation.

301
00:21:02,440 --> 00:21:04,430
Then the physics met an experiment.

302
00:21:04,480 --> 00:21:10,070
In 2020, a team deep underground at
Gran Sasso in Italy looked for the faint

303
00:21:10,120 --> 00:21:14,980
radiation that the simplest gravity-related
collapse model predicts, and found none.

304
00:21:15,600 --> 00:21:20,200
A 2022 follow-up argued that under
that simplest model, Orch O.R.

305
00:21:20,640 --> 00:21:24,350
becomes highly implausible, though
more flexible versions survive.

306
00:21:24,400 --> 00:21:30,490
On the biological side, a 2024 study found
that rats given a microtubule-stabilizing

307
00:21:30,540 --> 00:21:34,580
drug took about a minute longer to lose
their righting reflex under anesthesia:

308
00:21:35,180 --> 00:21:39,870
suggestive, but only eight treated rats,
no quantum effects measured, and an

309
00:21:39,920 --> 00:21:41,540
anesthetic with many other targets.

310
00:21:42,080 --> 00:21:47,530
In 2025, the study's senior author published
a review arguing that the experimental

311
00:21:47,580 --> 00:21:52,030
case is now strong. Most
neuroscientists remain unconvinced.

312
00:21:52,080 --> 00:21:54,540
There are more cautious quantum proposals.

313
00:21:55,040 --> 00:21:59,390
Chalmers and the philosopher Kelvin
McQueen have explored whether consciousness

314
00:21:59,440 --> 00:22:02,130
could cause collapse, combining I.I.T.

315
00:22:02,180 --> 00:22:07,110
with a mathematical collapse model, and
argued that versions not already ruled out

316
00:22:07,160 --> 00:22:09,700
could in principle be
tested on quantum computers.

317
00:22:10,000 --> 00:22:15,300
And Tegmark himself proposed, in 2014,
treating consciousness as a state of matter,

318
00:22:15,620 --> 00:22:20,290
which he nicknamed perceptronium, defined
by how it processes information rather

319
00:22:20,340 --> 00:22:23,090
than by quantum effects in neurons.

320
00:22:23,140 --> 00:22:26,600
Orch O.R.'s verdict on
machines is the clearest:

321
00:22:27,000 --> 00:22:31,590
classical computers can't be conscious,
because consciousness requires a physical

322
00:22:31,640 --> 00:22:33,140
process they don't perform.

323
00:22:34,360 --> 00:22:36,480
Finally, a position about positions.

324
00:22:36,920 --> 00:22:39,340
Eric Schwitzgebel calls it crazyism.

325
00:22:39,560 --> 00:22:43,810
Every theory of consciousness, he argues,
has at least one implication that sounds

326
00:22:43,860 --> 00:22:48,030
bizarre. Functionalism makes the
China brain conscious, I.I.T.

327
00:22:48,080 --> 00:22:51,020
certain logic-gate grids,
panpsychism electrons.

328
00:22:52,080 --> 00:22:56,310
Illusionism denies what seems undeniable,
and dualism adds a new ingredient to

329
00:22:56,360 --> 00:22:58,840
physics. Something crazy must be true;

330
00:22:59,040 --> 00:23:01,000
we just don't know which crazy thing.

331
00:23:01,180 --> 00:23:06,090
His paper, If Materialism Is True, the
United States Is Probably Conscious, makes

332
00:23:06,140 --> 00:23:08,600
the point with the China
brain's real-world cousin:

333
00:23:08,860 --> 00:23:13,510
by criteria liberal enough to include
rabbits and intelligent aliens, the United

334
00:23:13,560 --> 00:23:18,610
States, a richly integrated, self-monitoring,
goal-pursuing system, would seem to

335
00:23:18,660 --> 00:23:22,960
qualify. I.I.T. disagrees, thanks
to its exclusion principle.

336
00:23:23,420 --> 00:23:27,800
Most readers find the conclusion
unbelievable, which is precisely his point.

337
00:23:28,760 --> 00:23:32,460
Give me the scoreboard. Which
positions let machines in?

338
00:23:33,300 --> 00:23:37,480
Functionalism: yes, with the right
organization, and the fight is over grain.

339
00:23:38,820 --> 00:23:43,090
Representationalism: possibly, if the
machine's representations are genuinely about

340
00:23:43,140 --> 00:23:45,490
the world, which may depend
on its training history.

341
00:23:45,540 --> 00:23:50,350
Illusionism: yes, if it has the right
self-model for the right reasons.

342
00:23:50,400 --> 00:23:52,830
Chalmers's naturalistic dualism:

343
00:23:52,880 --> 00:23:55,570
yes, by organizational invariance.

344
00:23:55,620 --> 00:23:58,310
Enactivism and biological naturalism:

345
00:23:58,360 --> 00:24:00,610
unlikely without something like life.

346
00:24:00,660 --> 00:24:04,910
I.I.T.: not on conventional
digital hardware, whatever it does.

347
00:24:04,960 --> 00:24:09,030
Panpsychism: undetermined,
pending a combination principle.

348
00:24:09,080 --> 00:24:11,870
Kastrup's idealism: probably not.

349
00:24:11,920 --> 00:24:15,670
Orch O.R.: not for classical computers.

350
00:24:15,720 --> 00:24:17,300
Mysterianism: we may never know.

351
00:24:17,560 --> 00:24:22,320
And the octopus? Most positions on the
map let it in, which is worth noticing.

352
00:24:22,740 --> 00:24:25,980
The disagreements cluster
at machines, not at animals.

353
00:24:26,460 --> 00:24:31,740
Two more things. The positions that say
yes don't all say yes to the same systems:

354
00:24:32,120 --> 00:24:36,500
a functionalist and an illusionist could
disagree about a given language model.

355
00:24:36,720 --> 00:24:41,260
And the dividing line isn't between
scientific and unscientific views.

356
00:24:41,520 --> 00:24:44,400
It runs through the middle
of the scientific community.

357
00:24:45,540 --> 00:24:48,680
Before we leave the map, a
short detour, clearly marked:

358
00:24:48,860 --> 00:24:53,830
the edges. These are ideas that are
popular, sometimes striking, and often

359
00:24:53,880 --> 00:24:58,930
recommended in books and podcasts, but
that sit outside mainstream science or have

360
00:24:58,980 --> 00:25:00,680
little supporting evidence so far.

361
00:25:00,880 --> 00:25:05,270
For each, I'll give the claim and its
status, without mockery and without

362
00:25:05,320 --> 00:25:10,540
endorsement. Federico Faggin led the design
of the first commercial microprocessor.

363
00:25:10,860 --> 00:25:17,150
In his 2024 book Irreducible, and with
the physicist Giacomo Mauro D'Ariano, he

364
00:25:17,200 --> 00:25:21,830
argues that consciousness and free will are
fundamental, and that experience belongs

365
00:25:21,880 --> 00:25:27,350
to quantum systems in pure states, whose
information, like experience, is private

366
00:25:27,400 --> 00:25:31,520
and cannot be copied. So classical
computers can never be conscious.

367
00:25:32,140 --> 00:25:34,180
Status: a theoretical proposal.

368
00:25:34,520 --> 00:25:38,800
Its authors have sketched possible
experiments, but none has been carried out.

369
00:25:39,100 --> 00:25:44,210
From the Qualia Research Institute comes
the symmetry theory of valence, proposed by

370
00:25:44,260 --> 00:25:47,320
Michael Johnson in
Principia Qualia in 2016:

371
00:25:47,680 --> 00:25:52,490
how good or bad an experience feels is
encoded in the symmetry of the mathematical

372
00:25:52,540 --> 00:25:57,480
object that describes it, with harmony
as pleasure and dissonance as suffering.

373
00:25:57,660 --> 00:26:02,790
Its proponents have proposed brain-imaging
tests based on the harmonics of neural

374
00:26:02,840 --> 00:26:06,760
activity. Status: a conjecture
without independent confirmation.

375
00:26:07,300 --> 00:26:12,570
Nearby is David Pearce's non-materialist
physicalism, which holds that the intrinsic

376
00:26:12,620 --> 00:26:17,230
nature of the physical is experiential,
and that the unity of experience comes from

377
00:26:17,280 --> 00:26:21,450
quantum coherence in neurons over
extraordinarily short timescales.

378
00:26:21,500 --> 00:26:25,900
Pearce proposes a test that current
technology cannot yet perform.

379
00:26:26,340 --> 00:26:29,060
Status: speculative and untested.

380
00:26:29,280 --> 00:26:34,090
The cognitive scientist Donald Hoffman
argues in The Case Against Reality that

381
00:26:34,140 --> 00:26:38,770
evolution tunes perception to fitness
rather than truth, backed by published

382
00:26:38,820 --> 00:26:41,360
simulations that others
continue to debate.

383
00:26:41,820 --> 00:26:46,270
Beyond that, he proposes that reality
is fundamentally a network of conscious

384
00:26:46,320 --> 00:26:50,520
agents. Status: the simulations
are published science;

385
00:26:50,800 --> 00:26:54,140
the conscious-agent theory has
no confirmed predictions yet.

386
00:26:54,520 --> 00:27:00,210
Thomas Campbell, a physicist, argues in
his self-published trilogy My Big TOE that

387
00:27:00,260 --> 00:27:04,380
physical reality is a virtual reality
within a larger consciousness system.

388
00:27:04,640 --> 00:27:10,430
He has proposed variations of the double-slit
experiment as tests, and in 2024 a

389
00:27:10,480 --> 00:27:14,480
nonprofit he founded announced that
researchers had begun the first of them.

390
00:27:14,960 --> 00:27:18,240
Status: no peer-reviewed
results that I could find.

391
00:27:18,920 --> 00:27:24,330
Itzhak Bentov's 1977 book Stalking the
Wild Pendulum describes consciousness in

392
00:27:24,380 --> 00:27:27,180
terms of vibration and
oscillation underlying matter.

393
00:27:27,840 --> 00:27:32,600
Status: a popular, speculative book that
was never developed into a testable theory.

394
00:27:32,960 --> 00:27:37,470
Ken Wilber's integral theory maps
consciousness, culture, and contemplative

395
00:27:37,520 --> 00:27:39,340
development into quadrants and stages.

396
00:27:39,960 --> 00:27:44,840
Status: a philosophical and spiritual
synthesis, not an empirical theory.

397
00:27:45,220 --> 00:27:50,440
Two historical entries. The physicist
Wolfgang Pauli and the psychiatrist Carl Jung,

398
00:27:50,720 --> 00:27:55,310
over decades of correspondence, explored
the idea that mind and matter are two

399
00:27:55,360 --> 00:28:00,560
aspects of a single underlying reality,
along with Jung's notion of synchronicity.

400
00:28:01,020 --> 00:28:06,780
Status: a serious subject for historians
and philosophers, not an empirical theory.

401
00:28:07,040 --> 00:28:12,390
And Julian Jaynes's 1976 book on the
bicameral mind argues that introspective

402
00:28:12,440 --> 00:28:17,130
consciousness arose only about three
thousand years ago, and that earlier people

403
00:28:17,180 --> 00:28:20,800
experienced one hemisphere's
commands as the voices of gods.

404
00:28:21,360 --> 00:28:24,140
Status: admired as a provocation.

405
00:28:24,480 --> 00:28:29,230
Its historical claims are widely disputed,
though its emphasis on language in

406
00:28:29,280 --> 00:28:31,300
shaping introspection has defenders.

407
00:28:31,660 --> 00:28:36,830
What these ideas share is ambition, and
the test for each is the same one we apply

408
00:28:36,880 --> 00:28:40,600
to the mainstream: a prediction
that someone could actually check.

409
00:28:40,920 --> 00:28:46,660
That ends the detour. So in the next two
episodes, we turn to what can be checked.

410
00:28:47,040 --> 00:28:52,250
First the evidence: patients, experiments,
altered states, animals, and the

411
00:28:52,300 --> 00:28:56,540
remarkable tools that can now detect
consciousness in people who cannot move.

412
00:28:56,740 --> 00:28:59,580
Then the theories, put on
trial against each other.
